# 0065-pause-cancel-covers.patch -- Pause, Cancel and Resume of a running build; a Switch 2 package's own icon (.nup); "Choose a cover…" for any game.
# Applies after 0064 (the tree with 0010..0064 applied). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled (dotnet build, ios-arm64, Release: rc 0, no error; 47 warnings against 45 for the tree without it: two more call sites of the
# CA1416 BGContinuedProcessingTask.UpdateTitle warning GameAppBackground.cs already has) and the pure classes run in a host harness (a net console program built from the real source text of GameAppStop.cs, GameAppCover.cs,
# GameAppQueue.cs, GameAppUi.cs and GameAppPerf.cs: 209 checks, 5 mutations of the classes each caught); no iPhone run. Measured and assumed are told apart below.
#
# WHY (the owner's requests, and the field facts of the 22,000-shader build on the iPhone): the owner asked for Pause and Cancel of a build and for a 'Choose a cover' option; the Switch 2 package of Zelda Ocarina of Time has no
#   icon at all (no NACP, no icon in its metadata), so it can only show its initials. A handler must never walk or delete a big folder on the main thread (0x8BADF00D, patch 0064): nothing here does; the sizes in the cancel dialog
#   are the cached FolderSizes, the cleanup is DeleteFolderInBackground, the request is raised from a pool thread, the decoding of a photo is on a worker.
#
# FEATURE A -- PAUSE / CANCEL / RESUME (the Rust side is done: gameapp_request_stop(kind) 1 = pause, 2 = cancel; gameapp_run_chain() returns 131 paused / 130 cancelled, which is not a failure):
#   * GameAppStop.cs (new; no UIKit, run by the harness): StopMachine, the state machine (Idle, Running, Pausing, Paused, Cancelling, Cancelled; every transition returns what the caller must DO and calls nothing itself), StopText
#       (the words), CancelPlan (what a cancel deletes: only what the build made; never shadercache/, games/<id>/, the game file, the finished shaders/<id>), StopFiles / PausedStore / StopRecovery (the paused state rebuilt from
#       the files at launch), ResumePlan (Continue on extract/ with the continuation file, or from the start), QueueStartPlan.
#   * GameAppStopUi.cs (new): the buttons, the dialog, the resume, the Build tab card, the launch reconstruction, the system's task. The chain clears a request when a run starts, so a Pause or Cancel pressed BEFORE the chain is on the
#       stack (the C# extraction of an NSP/XCI runs first, in the same process) is kept in the StopMachine and applied before gameapp_run_chain is called: the chain is NOT started, the run ends as paused / cancelled at once; one raised
#       in the microseconds around the chain's own clear is raised again every 2 s while the stop is honoured (a Timer; raising twice is harmless).
#       Pause (build-pause): the card says 'Pausing… (finishes the current shader)' (or '(finishes the current step)', '(finishes reading the game)' during the extraction), both buttons off. When the chain returns 131: the record
#       Documents/gameapp.paused.tsv (game, id, whether extract/ holds it, stage number, time, the last stage line: the chain's own marker shaders/<id>.work/stopped.by.user exists only when stage 3 was cut), the queue item
#       becomes 'paused' (the queue stops by itself), the continued-processing task is completed with the title 'Paused: <game>'; no 'build failed' line, no notification, no gameapp.killed.
#       Paused: a card 'Paused · 14,990 of 21,990 shaders' with Resume (build-resume) and Cancel (build-cancel), a 'Paused · 75%' chip on the library tile and row, 'Paused · 14,990 of 21,990 shaders' on the Build tab's game row.
#       Resume = the chain again: Continue (StartContinuedBuild on the files in extract/, which writes Documents/gameapp.continue.run: game=<file>, stage=N, why=paused; a Switch 2 package is not read again) or, when extract/ is not
#       that game's any more (another game was built meanwhile) or the package read itself was cut, from the start (the shaders already finished still come from the cache). NEVER gameapp.continue, except when a run already
#       happened in this process and the person confirms the restart ('GameApp starts again': the file the launch already reads). After a stop (130 / 131) pythonRanThisProcess is cleared: the shim makes and destroys a handle
#       at every run_chain, so a second run in the process is possible (the Rust agent's tests); not exercised on the phone.
#       Cancel (build-cancel): a dialog 'Cancel the build of <game>?' with 'Cancel and keep the extracted game (next build starts faster)' (deletes only shaders/<id>.work) and 'Cancel and free the space (N GB)' (also extract/ and a
#       partial framework folder; N from the cached extract + shaders-work sizes, 'at least' when one is not known, no number when none is) and 'Keep building'. Both run on DeleteFolderInBackground (one at a time, nothing measured
#       meanwhile, BeginRun refuses meanwhile), then the status is 'Cancelled' (the Build tab row's chip, the build screen). The cancel removes the item from the queue and stops the queue. A cancel from the paused state is the same
#       without a chain. A stop that came after the chain had finished is no stop: the build is finished and nothing is deleted.
#   * The paused state survives a relaunch: RecoverStoppedBuilds (launch-profile part 'RecoverStoppedBuilds') rebuilds it from the files: the marker says 'pause' (or the app's own list has the game: a pause before stage 3 leaves
#       no marker), gameapp.killed does not name the game (iOS ended that run: the 'interrupted, resume' flow stays as it is) and its framework is not built. A marker that says 'cancel' is a cancel whose cleanup never finished:
#       its work folder is removed in the background. A deleted game loses its record.
#   * Queue (GameAppQueue.cs, GameAppQueueUi.cs): a 'paused' state; Next skips it (nothing restarts it by itself); 'Start the queue' with a paused build asks ('<game> is paused: Resume <game> / Start without it / Not now');
#       Resume continues the queue; cancel removes the item and stops the queue; the queue page and its status line say so.
#   * The iOS 26 continued-processing task (GameAppBackground.cs): when the system expires it (or the person taps its stop button in the Dynamic Island) a BUILD's task is treated as a PAUSE: the request is raised, the wait for the
#       chain to return and for the record is bounded (5 s) and on a pool thread, then SetTaskCompleted(true) with the title 'Paused: <game>'; never on the main thread. A warm-up's task, or a build still reading its game, keeps
#       the old behaviour (the work goes on as far as iOS lets the process live).
#   * The build screen (GameAppLibrary.cs, GameBuildViewController): Pause and Cancel under the live log while it runs, 'PAUSED' / Resume / Cancel when paused, 'CANCELLED'; its steps are not marked done.
#   * Hooks in GameAppMain.cs: BeginRun (StopBegin; ChainStarting before gameapp_run_chain; ChainReturned after it: 130 / 131 are not failures), Finish (StopEnding, BackgroundEnd(paused), no notification for a stop), the 1 s ticker
#       (the card's clock and 'Pausing…'), a deleted game's record, the launch.
#
# FEATURE B -- the NUP icon (shim export int gameapp_nup_icon(const char *path, uint8_t *buf, int cap, uint64_t *title_id), Rust: crates/ibuilder-nup icon.rs + ibuilder-ffi, by the perf agent):
#   * GameAppCoverUi.cs FetchMissingNupIcons (called from RefreshScreen next to FetchMissingIcons, which keeps skipping .nup and needs the keys a Switch 2 package does not): a background task, not while a build runs, one package at a
#       time: the icon (a 1 MiB buffer, asked again with the length when it is bigger) is written to games/<id>/icon.jpg through icon.jpg.part; the package's title id names the game when its file name has none (the same
#       SetTitleFromContent / SaveTitlesFromContent the NSP path uses, id & ~0xFFF); no icon (rc 0: the owner's Ocarina package) or a damaged package is recorded as games/<id>/icon.none (games/_unresolved/<hash>.none without an id),
#       and tried again only when the file is newer than the note. Reads only the head of the file. The owner's Ocarina package has no icon: its result is 'none' and the initials stay. Part 'nup icons/worker' of the launch profile.
#
# FEATURE C -- CHOOSE A COVER (GameAppCover.cs, no UIKit, run by the harness; GameAppCoverUi.cs):
#   * games/<id>/cover.jpg (512 x 512, JPEG 0.9), a file of its own: the chain writes games/<id>/icon.jpg at every build and never touches cover.jpg. A game with no [title id] in its name and none read yet: games/_unresolved/
#       <hash>.cover.jpg (the hash GameAppUi.UnresolvedMarker uses); once the id is read the cover is found at the old key too and a new choice goes under the id.
#   * Precedence: cover.jpg, then icon.jpg (then the extraction's icon, then the initials): CoverOf, so the tint (Theme.AverageColor), the tiles, the rows, the sheet's header and the Build tab follow; the exported player's own
#       screen (GameCover.PlayerCandidates: its Documents' cover, the bundle's, then the icons). GameAppExport.cs: the app icon is made from the cover when there is one; games/<id>/ travels whole, and a cover kept under the file
#       name's key is added as documents/games/<id>/cover.jpg. Deleting a game removes its cover (GameAppUi.PlanDeletion; the saves stay).
#   * Where: the long-press menu of a Library tile and row ('Choose a cover…' submenu), the game sheet's GAME section (a row; it opens an action sheet), the Build tab row's More menu. Entries: 'Photo Library…' (PHPickerViewController,
#       images, one; no permission prompt: PhotosUI is a system process, nothing to add to Info.plist; the project already links PhotosUI through the bindings), 'Files…' (UIDocumentPickerViewController for public.image, asCopy:
#       the picker's copy in our container is deleted after the read), 'Remove custom cover' when one is set. The picture is decoded OFF the main thread: an ImageIO thumbnail (the orientation applied, no more than 1024 on the
#       short side), the centred square, 512 x 512, JPEG 0.9, written through cover.jpg.part and renamed. The launch sweep (SweepLeftoverParts) removes cover.jpg.part, icon.jpg.part and <hash>.cover.jpg.part in games/<id>/ and
#       games/_unresolved/ (one level, by exact name).
#
# NEW STRINGS: Pause, Resume, Cancel, 'Pausing… (finishes the current shader)', 'Pausing… (finishes the current step)', 'Pausing… (finishes reading the game)', 'Cancelling…', 'Cancelled', 'Paused', 'Paused · ' (card and chip),
#   'Cancel the build of ', 'Cancel and keep the extracted game (next build starts faster)', 'Cancel and free the space', 'Keep building', 'Not now', 'Cancel the paused build…', ' is paused', 'Start without it', 'Paused: '
#   (the task's title), 'GameApp starts again' (existing), 'Choose a cover…', 'Choose a cover', 'Photo Library…', 'Files…', 'Remove custom cover'. ACCESSIBILITY IDS: build-pause, build-resume, build-cancel (the Build tab card),
#   build-row-resume, build-row-cancel (a Build tab game row), game-resume (a library tile / row), build-screen-pause, build-screen-cancel (the build screen), sheet-cancel-build, cover-choose (the game sheet's row, and the
#   menu's identifier), cover-photos, cover-files, cover-remove (menu actions).
#
# MEASURED (host harness, .NET 11, Apple silicon Mac): 209 checks, every transition of the state machine (also the pending-before-start cases: a Pause or a Cancel during the extraction applies before the chain, a Pause then a
#   Cancel is a cancel), 20,000 races of a Pause against the chain's start (never a request lost), the cancel plans for 32 combinations (none lists shadercache/, games/, the finished pack, a game file or prod.keys; a paused build whose extraction another game replaced deletes only its own work folder) and EXECUTED on a
#   real folder tree (shader cache, games/<id>/ with a save and the rewrite cache, another game's work folder, the game file all survive), the relaunch reconstruction (killed, built, cancel marker, no id, two games, the round
#   trip), the resume plans, the queue, the cover paths and precedence, the crop and decode sizes. Mutations of the classes (the chain ignores a pending request / a cancel also deletes shadercache / recovery ignores
#   gameapp.killed / 131 as a failure / the icon beats the cover) fail the checks that name them. Compile: dotnet build -c Release -r ios-arm64, rc 0, links PhotosUI and UniformTypeIdentifiers.
# NOT EXERCISED / ASSUMED (no device run): everything UIKit (the card, the buttons, the dialog, the menus, the build screen, the pickers); the stop latency on the phone (the Rust agent's estimate is under 2 s: one translator call +
#   one shader; a module of the rewrite in hand, the split of the game's shaders and the C# extraction finish first, so a Pause there waits); a second gameapp_run_chain in the same process on the phone; the Dynamic Island's stop
#   button reaching ExpirationHandler (assumed: the system expires the task); BGContinuedProcessingTask.UpdateTitle after the expiration (wrapped in a try, not known to be honoured); PHPicker's LoadDataRepresentation giving
#   HEIC / JPEG / PNG bytes that ImageIO decodes with the orientation applied; the image processing itself (CoreGraphics is not in the harness: the crop and size arithmetic is); that gameapp_nup_icon links and answers on the
#   phone (the compile used stand-in exports: app-work/device's shim predates the two exports; the real ones are in app/gameapp_shim_rust.c, and build-app.sh's import check only reads the unpatched snapshot, so it does not
#   demand them: scripts/build-app.sh builds the shim from the repo's source and the link would fail loudly if they were missing).
# RISKS: Cancel after a Rebuild of a built game leaves that game needing setup (the chain removed its stamp and extract/flat when it started: nothing to restore); the 'free the space (N GB)' number is the cached extract +
#   shaders-work size, taken when the dialog opens (shaders-work is measured at launch, at the end of a build and by a delete: during a build it can be old); a pause during the C# extraction of a big NSP waits for it (it cannot be
#   interrupted); the paused record is the app's, the marker the chain's: if the two disagree (a marker with no record: progress unknown, 'Paused'; a record with no marker: a pause before stage 3) the safer reading is taken
#   (the extraction is not trusted, the game is read again); a Switch 2 title id whose low 12 bits are not 000 would be stored as id & ~0xFFF like an NSP's, and could differ from the chain's games/<id>/ name.
